= A Meeting in a Beer Hall

[? if bavaria_soviet = 2 : On 24 February, in a Nuremberg beer hall full of officers and students who fled red Munich, ?][? if bavaria_soviet != 2 : On 24 February, in the great hall of the Hofbräuhaus in Munich, ?] the little German Workers' Party holds its biggest meeting yet. Two thousand people come. A former corporal named Adolf Hitler reads out a programme of twenty-five points: against Versailles, against "interest slavery", against the Jews.

A few communists and Independents in the hall heckle and are thrown out. A week later the party renames itself the National Socialist German Workers' Party.

Our Munich comrades send a short report. Most of the Zentrale reads it and puts it aside.

- @ignore: A beer-hall sect. We have bigger enemies.
- @disrupt: Break up their meetings.
- @expose: Expose them in our press as the counter-revolution's new face.

@ignore
subtitle: Costs nothing now.
on-arrival: {!
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 1;
!}

There are a dozen such leagues in Bavaria alone. This one will fade like the rest, the Zentrale decides.

@disrupt
subtitle: The Left's answer. Brawls in the beer halls; the police take notes.
on-arrival: {!
Q.militancy += 3;
Q.repression += 2;
Q.district_bavaria_loyalty += 4;
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 4;
Q.lean_offensive += 1;
!}

Our young comrades start turning up at their meetings in groups. The Nazis answer by forming a "hall protection" squad of ex-soldiers.

@expose
subtitle: The Spartacist answer. Few readers care yet.
on-arrival: {!
Q.worker_support += 1;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 3;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 1;
!}

Our Munich paper prints the twenty-five points with a commentary: who pays for this "workers' party", and why its speakers never mention a single strike.
